# Formatted with ruff's default settings: uvx ruff format <this file>
# The shebang's "# python" is a comment env -S drops; editors read it to pick Python.
# Tests: pantheon_tests, next to this file.

"""Pantheon: agent collaboration and convergence.

Each agent is a real CLI (claude, codex, or gemini). A mode names its agents, and a plan: stages in
which the agents take turns, and in a loop, take them again until one of them finds the stage's
halting prompt true. A stage can also let its agents stop the plan, when they find it should not go
on. A multi agent has an instance on each track, one for each tool; pantheon start help MODE shows a
mode's agents and plan. Every agent knows the team, the plan, and the room, and acts when it sees a
reason; once the plan is done, the agents keep running, and you give them more work in the room.
Pantheon shows the team in a chat-room feed (the room), gives each agent a tab with a live copy of
its screen, and lets you "transport" into an agent's own terminal when it needs you. Ctrl-] or the
button on tmux's status line brings you back. The top row has a tab for each session you have open
({ and } switch them); the row below has its agents' tabs. pantheon help lists the commands.

Agents keep your own setup: agent files, skills, MCP servers, and permission rules. Pantheon adds
only rules that let them run the room's two commands without asking you. An ollama agent is claude
with the ollama server as its API; if claude keeps an API key from a Console login, it sends that
key to the server too. Pantheon types each turn's instructions straight into its agent, and the
agent writes its result to a file that ends with a "PANTHEON: <status>" line. The room is the team's
channel: each agent learns how to read it (pantheon room) and post to it (pantheon say), and
pantheon gives each post to every other agent, which decides whether it needs to act. So an agent
can ask the team a question, or wake another agent. You can post too (ctrl+s): to answer, or to give
a team that is done more work. Pantheon's own lines in the room go to no agent. Each session has a
private tmux server, so agents keep running when pantheon exits. Claude and codex report through
hooks; gemini reports through its window title. The room names the model each agent really runs, and
claude's effort, where they differ from what pantheon asked for (gemini's model comes from its chat
file). Unless it runs from a clone of gregschmit/dotfiles (git updates that), pantheon looks on
GitHub for a newer version at most every 12 hours.

Files: ~/.local/state/pantheon (or $PANTHEON_HOME) holds config.toml and one sqlite file per
session, sessions/<slug>.sqlite3. That file is the whole session: its record, its lock, the prompts,
and the results. Agents write plain files, so while they run, pantheon keeps their results in
run/<slug>/, and copies each result into the session file, and each later revision an agent makes to
it as a revised entry in the room. pantheon say leaves each post in run/<slug>/posts/ for pantheon
to give out.
"""

